mode: "100644"
text: "# The REVEAL framework — transparency that builds trust, not regret\n\nBehind-the-scenes content works when it's true, specific, and deliberately bounded. Each letter is a gate; the\ncounterfeits fail at R (didn't happen) or A (performed), and the career-enders fail at V (shouldn't have left\nthe building).\n\n## R — Root it in what actually happened\nBTS material is surfaced, never invented: this week's real work, a decision you actually made, a mistake you\nactually survived, a milestone you actually hit, a tool/process you actually use. Good prompts: *what did we\ndecide this week and what did it cost? · what broke? · what would a new hire be surprised by? · what does hour\ntwo of the workday actually look like?* If you can't point at the moment on a calendar, it isn't behind the\nscenes — it's fiction with a hoodie on.\n\n## E — Expose the specifics + stakes\nThe gap between a press release and BTS is specificity and stakes.\n- **Press release:** \"Excited to share we shipped v2 of our onboarding!\"\n- **Behind the scenes:** \"We rewrote onboarding twice. The first version tested great and tanked live — 43\n  refunds in a week. Here's the decision that fixed it.\"\nOne real number, one real tradeoff, one thing it cost you. The \"boring\" middle — the spreadsheet, the failed\ndraft, the pricing debate — is exactly what the audience never gets to see anywhere else. That's the asset.\n\n## V — Vet the transparency tier (the gate that keeps you employed and unsued)\nThree tiers; the human makes the call, and the call is permanent:\n- **Share freely:** process, decisions + reasoning, tools, lessons, resolved mistakes, culture-in-action,\n  day-in-the-life, launches and the mess behind them.\n- **Handle with care (deliberate, owned choice):** revenue/MRR (once you start, stopping reads as bad news),\n  pricing logic, ongoing struggles (share from a stable footing, not mid-freefall), personal health/family.\n- **Never:** customer data or identifiable customer stories without explicit consent · team conflicts,\n  performance issues, firings, or anything about a named/identifiable employee · active legal, regulatory, or\n  dispute matters · security, credentials, or exploitable operational detail · numbers you're contractually or\n  legally bound to keep private.\n**The permanence test:** would you be fine with this post on screen in a lawsuit, a hiring call, and your\nbiggest customer's inbox? All three, or it doesn't ship. Regulated industries (finance/health/legal): revenue\nand results claims go through compliance first.\n\n## E — Establish whose voice\n- **Founder-voice:** first person, from the personal account, owns \"I\", includes the doubt and the decision.\n  This is where the reach and trust live — but only if it's actually the founder's voice and feelings. The agent\n  drafts in that voice; it never ghost-writes emotions the founder doesn't have.\n- **Brand-voice:** the company account curates — \"we\", team culture, process, how-it's-made. Warmer than a press\n  release, but it doesn't perform intimacy it can't have.\nPick one per piece. A brand account doing first-person confession is as off as a founder doing corporate-speak.\n\n## A — Avoid the counterfeits\n- **Manufactured vulnerability:** the engineered tear-jerker (\"I cried in my car. Then I closed the deal.\") —\n  the genre's defining cringe; audiences smell it and trust dies (`linkedin-post-writer`'s avoiding-cringe file\n  is the calibration).\n- **The humble-brag:** a \"mistake\" that's secretly a flex (\"my biggest flaw? I care too much\"). A real mistake\n  post names what it cost and what you'd do differently.\n- **Struggle-porn:** raw pain shared for reach while you're still inside it. Share struggles from a stable\n  footing, with a lesson or an honest \"here's where we are\" — not as a live breakdown.\n- **The fabricated parable:** the homeless-man-taught-me-marketing genre. If it didn't happen, it doesn't post.\n\n## L — Loop it into a cadence\nBuild-in-public is a rhythm, not a stunt. Commit to a sustainable cadence (weekly or biweekly beats daily-then-\nvanish), decide the recurring beats up front (metrics update, decision log, lesson, ship note), and **share the\nmiddle, not just wins** — an all-wins feed is just marketing with extra steps. Serialize into the\n`content-calendar`, route cuts to the format writers (`caption-writer`, `linkedin-post-writer`,\n`short-form-video-script`, `story-writer`, `thread-writer`), publish via `scheduling-and-queue` → WoopSocial,\nand have the founder show up in the replies — the comments are where the trust actually compounds.\n\n## The gut check before you post\nThree questions: (1) Did this actually happen, exactly as written? (2) Does it pass the permanence test —\nlawsuit, hiring call, customer's inbox? (3) Am I sharing this because it's useful/true, or performing it for\nreach? If any answer is wrong, fix the reveal — don't polish the performance.\n"
